hould calculate next number: how many ways to set to distances between segments? It's clear, that we can increase distance between some segments, but we have only "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such operations. It's well known, that answer number of ways equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). So, for each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we should sum by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" following values: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sum"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note, that we need array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] (binomials) where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 2000, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 2000."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/403/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"403E - Two Rooted Trees"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, for each vertex of the first and second tree we calculate two values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"out"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] — the entry time and exit time in dfs order from vertex with number 1. Also with each edge from both trees we will assosiate a pair ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]), "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]) (values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"out"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for each vertex we take from tree with another color). Now for each tree we will build two segment trees (yes, totally 4 segment trees). In first segment will store all pairs in following way: we will store a pair in node of segment tree if and only if (node of segment tree is a segment ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) left element of pair lies in segment ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). In second segment tree we will store a pair if and only if right element of the pair lies in segment ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") All pairs in segment trees we will store in some order (in first segment tree — increasing order, in the second tree — decreasing order). Such trees use "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/cc3c1ecb1b0469c6167f8e23f30d6c7baf9660af42fe8381854b550eac1ce3cc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" of memory, also you can build it in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/cc3c1ecb1b0469c6167f8e23f30d6c7baf9660af42fe8381854b550eac1ce3cc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Good. How to answer on query ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") — erase all edges from tree for which exactly one vertex have value "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"in"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] in segment ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")? We will go down in our segment tree. Let's imagine, that now we in some node of segment tree. Because we store all pairs in the first segment tree in increasing order of the right element, so answer to the query is a some suffix of the array of pairs. After we can add they to the answer (if it not erased yet). After that we should modify our segment tree: for each node, where we work with suffixes, we should erase all pairs from such suffix. So, this solution in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/cc3c1ecb1b0469c6167f8e23f30d6c7baf9660af42fe8381854b550eac1ce3cc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"My solution to E: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/403/submission/6052738"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6052738"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/10972"}